{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If the anointed priest, etc.</b> He began with the priest and not with the congregation — and all the more so since He said <b>to the guilt of the people</b>, which likens his inadvertent sin to the inadvertent sin of the people, and it would be fitting to give it precedence at the outset. Or perhaps it comes to say that the offering of the anointed one must precede that of the congregation, and needless to say that of the king, and the reason is as they said in tractate Horayot (Horayot 13a): the priest effects atonement and the congregation receives atonement. And I found that our Sages of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim on verse 13) said: the bull of the anointed priest and the bull of the congregation — the bull of the anointed priest comes first, and they derived it from the verse ״וְאִם כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. — from this you learn that it must be given precedence. They further derived from the verse ״כַּאֲשֶׁר שָׂרַף״ etc. — from here that the bull of the anointed one must precede the bull of the congregation." "And one must know why it was not sufficient for our Sages of blessed memory to learn the precedence from the fact that Scripture itself placed it first — that they needed to search for other derivations. Or perhaps it is that if there were nothing but the precedence in order alone, I would say that Scripture did not arrange it before the congregation's offering except for the reason that if it had arranged it after the congregation's offering, His saying ״אִם הַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ יֶחֱטָא לְאַשְׁמַת הָעָם״ would be understood to mean what is stated nearby — that the court ruled and he ruled together with them — but this is not so, for if he ruled together with the congregation he is atoned together with the congregation, as is found in the Mishnah (Horayot 7a). Therefore He went ahead and arranged his case separately. Yet I would still say that his offering does not precede that of the congregation; therefore they derived it from the verse ״וְאִם״ etc. And it is still difficult why two derivations were needed for this — the derivation from ״וְאִם״ and the derivation from ״כַּאֲשֶׁר שָׂרַף״ (Leviticus 4:21). And it appears that one is for optional [situations] and one for obligatory [situations]. As for what I saw that the author of Korban Aharon gave as a reason — that I would have said it applies to a part and not to the whole — there is no substance to his reason. It further appears possible to say: one is for if he preceded and brought [his offering] and they had not yet brought the bull of the congregation and brought it afterward, so that both of them are standing [available]; and one is for if the bull of the anointed one and the bull of the congregation came at once — even so, the one of the anointed takes precedence." "<b>To the guilt of the people.</b> It may be explained along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Avot 5:18): one who brings merit to the many — no sin comes through him. And upon seeing that the anointed priest sinned — this tells that those whom he merited did not merit sufficiently, so that no sin would come through him on their account. And this is what He says: <b>to the guilt of the people.</b>" "And in Torat Kohanim they derived that by <b>לְאַשְׁמַת הָעָם</b> Scripture intends to indicate the type of sin for which one becomes liable to bring an offering, namely concealment of the matter together with inadvertent action, as it says: ״לְאַשְׁמַת הָעָם״ (Leviticus 4:13) ״וְנֶעְלַם דָּבָר״ etc. And if the anointed priest inadvertently sinned in inadvertent action alone, the matter depends on whether he is liable to bring the offering of a commoner or not — and our Sages of blessed memory derived (Horayot 11a) from the verse ״מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ״ to exclude the anointed priest, for he is entirely exempt. And if the anointed priest is not a peerless sage, he is exempt (Horayot 7a) from every type of offering — whether for concealment of the matter with inadvertent action, or for inadvertent action alone — and one must give a reason for this thing. Or perhaps it is because one who acts in concealment of the matter and is a peerless sage — this inadvertence makes a great impression upon his soul and distances it from its root, and the merit of Israel, for whom he effects atonement, cannot avail to prevent the blemish from reaching him. This is not so for one who sinned in action alone, or in concealment of the matter but is not a peerless sage — this sin does not produce a blemish in the aspect of the soul of the anointed priest to distance it from its root such that it would require an offering to draw it near; for through the mediation of the merit of the many, El mighty does not reject [him]. Nor would this suffice as a sign that the people are guilty, from the fact that this inadvertent sinner who is the anointed one stumbled — for the reason itself that we have written."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:07.174946574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}